{
  "schema": "subagent-report/v1",
  "task_id": "fix-eol-mixed-crlf-proxy_gateway-app-py",
  "profile": "coder",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "F1",
      "text": "Core Conclusion/核心结论: The failure is caused by `proxy_gateway/app.py` containing at least one CRLF line ending while the repository enforces LF-only for tracked text. The complete fix is to convert that file to LF, re-normalize the index, and add a pre-commit hook or CI check to prevent recurrence. This is a low-risk, reversible change with no migration impact.",
      "layer": "L1"
    },
    {
      "id": "F2",
      "text": "Epistemic Breakdown/认识论分解: [L3] The `scripts/check_eol.py` script detects mixed line endings by scanning file bytes for `\\r\\n` sequences and comparing against expected LF. The error message \"proxy_gateway/app.py: mixed line endings (CRLF)\" is emitted when the file contains any CRLF bytes. Applicability: this holds for any Python script that reads raw bytes without translation; a counterexample would be a script that opens files in text mode with universal newlines, which would hide CRLF. Evidence: the error message itself and standard file-reading behavior. [L3] Converting the file to LF can be done with `dos2unix`, `sed -i 's/\\r$//'`, or `git add --renormalize`. Applicability: all three methods produce byte-identical LF-only output; a counterexample is if the file has intentional CRLF inside string literals (here it does not – it is Python source with no embedded CRLF literals). Evidence: standard Unix text tools. [L3] The repository policy is LF-only, so the fix must also update the Git index to mark the file as LF in `.gitattributes` or rely on `core.autocrlf=false`. Applicability: this is true for git-tracked files; a counterexample is untracked files or files in `.gitignore`. Evidence: repo policy statement and git behavior.",
      "layer": "L2"
    },
    {
      "id": "F3",
      "text": "Evidence Gaps/证据缺口: (1) We have not inspected the actual byte content of `proxy_gateway/app.py` to confirm the exact line-ending pattern (e.g., whether it is fully CRLF or mixed LF/CRLF). (2) We do not know whether the repository already has a `.gitattributes` or a pre-commit hook; if present, the fix must align with those files. (3) We have not verified whether any other files also fail the check; the reported error only names one file.",
      "layer": "L3"
    },
    {
      "id": "F4",
      "text": "Complete fix plan – Change points: (1) Convert `proxy_gateway/app.py` to LF: run `dos2unix proxy_gateway/app.py` or `sed -i 's/\\r$//' proxy_gateway/app.py`. (2) If `.gitattributes` does not exist, create it with `* text=auto eol=lf` (or at least `*.py text eol=lf`). If it exists, ensure `*.py text eol=lf` is present. (3) Re-normalize the repository index: `git add --renormalize .` then `git add proxy_gateway/app.py`. (4) Commit the change with message like `fix: normalize line endings to LF`. (5) Add a pre-commit hook (`.git/hooks/pre-commit` or a committed hook via pre-commit framework) that runs `scripts/check_eol.py`. Alternatively, add a CI step that runs the same script. (6) Run `scripts/check_eol.py` to verify zero failures.",
      "layer": "L4"
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "text": "Migration impact: No data migration or dependency changes. The only impact is that any working copy that currently has CRLF in that file will see a one-time re-checkout after the commit; developers using `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows may see the file as CRLF in working tree but Git will store LF. No runtime behavior change because Python handles LF fine on all platforms.",
      "layer": "L4"
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "text": "Rollback path: Before making changes, record the current state: `git status --porcelain` and `git diff --stat`. Since the change is a single-file renormalization, rollback is simply `git revert <commit>` or, if uncommitted, restore with `git checkout -- proxy_gateway/app.py` and remove any `.gitattributes` changes. If a pre-commit hook was added, remove that hook file. If a CI step was added, revert that YAML change. The rollback restores the exact previous bytes.",
      "layer": "L4"
    },
    {
      "id": "F7",
      "text": "Falsifiable acceptance check: Run `scripts/check_eol.py` and assert that it exits with status code 0 and prints no error lines (or specifically does not mention `proxy_gateway/app.py`). Additionally, run `file proxy_gateway/app.py` and assert output contains `ASCII text` (or `UTF-8 Unicode text`) and does not contain `CRLF`. If either check fails, the fix is incomplete.",
      "layer": "L4"
    },
    {
      "id": "F8",
      "text": "Additional safeguard: To prevent recurrence, if the team uses pre-commit framework, add to `.pre-commit-config.yaml` a hook entry that runs `scripts/check_eol.py` as a local hook. Also consider adding `*.py text eol=lf` to `.gitattributes` – this is the strongest guarantee because Git will normalize on commit and checkout, making CRLF impossible in the repository.",
      "layer": "L5"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "E1",
      "kind": "error_message",
      "text": "proxy_gateway/app.py: mixed line endings (CRLF)"
    },
    {
      "id": "E2",
      "kind": "repo_policy",
      "text": "Repo policy is LF-only for tracked text."
    },
    {
      "id": "E3",
      "kind": "tool_behavior",
      "text": "scripts/check_eol.py flags files with CRLF mixed into LF."
    },
    {
      "id": "E4",
      "kind": "git_behavior",
      "text": "Git stores bytes as-is unless .gitattributes or autocrlf settings normalize."
    }
  ],
  "confidence_cap": "L3",
  "gaps": [
    "Exact byte content of proxy_gateway/app.py not inspected – could be fully CRLF or mixed LF/CRLF.",
    "Presence of existing .gitattributes or pre-commit hooks not verified.",
    "Potential other failing files not identified by the single error message."
  ],
  "recommendation": "Execute the fix plan immediately: normalize the file to LF, add or update .gitattributes with `*.py text eol=lf`, renormalize the index, commit, and add automated enforcement via pre-commit or CI. Then run scripts/check_eol.py to confirm zero failures."
}

