    {
      "id": "L3-06",
      "text": "Claim: The script scripts/check_eol.py currently exits non-zero only when it finds mixed endings, but not necessarily when the whole file is CRLF. Applicability: This is inferred from the error message. If the script's policy is 'LF-only', then a fully CRLF file should also fail. Counterexample: If the script is intentionally lenient and only reports mixed as a warning, then changing it to strict failure is a small behavior change; the acceptance check must cover both cases. Verification: Inspect the script's exit code logic before modifying.",
      "layer": "L3",
      "confidence": 0.70
    },
    {
      "id": "L4-07",
      "text": "Evidence Gaps/证据缺口: (1) The exact list of files with CRLF is unknown — the error only mentions app.py. (2) The current content of app.py around the CRLF occurrences is not inspected, so we don't know if a string literal contains `\\r`. (3) The script's detection logic and exit codes are not fully known. (4) Whether .gitattributes exists and its current contents are unknown. (5) The exact Git client configuration on contributors' machines is unknown (autocrlf settings). These gaps must be closed before or during the fix, not after.",
      "layer": "L4",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "id": "L5-08",
      "text": "Recommendation: Do not use `git add --renormalize` alone, because it requires .gitattributes to have a text attribute set first. Instead: Step 1: Inspect repo state — `git ls-files > /tmp/tracked.txt`, `git grep -Il $'\\r' $(git ls-files)` to list all files with CRLF. Step 2: Add or update .gitattributes to enforce `* text=auto` and specific `*.py text eol=lf`. Step 3: Normalize tracked files: for each file in the list from Step 1, run `sed -i 's/\\r$//'` (but first back up any file that may contain intentional `\\r` in strings; use `grep -n $'\\r'` and eyeball). Step 4: Update scripts/check_eol.py to enforce strict LF-only: exit non-zero on any CRLF, not just mixed. Step 5: Add pre-commit hook (e.g., a simple git pre-commit that runs the checker) and/or CI step. Step 6: Commit with message like 'fix: normalize line endings to LF'. Migration impact: zero functional behavior change; developers on Windows need `git config core.autocrlf false` or the .gitattributes will handle normalization on check-in/out. Rollback: `git revert <commit>` or `git reset --hard HEAD~1` will restore the original blobs (CRLF) exactly, because Git stores the pre-normalization blob if the commit is reverted; if .gitattributes changed, also revert that file. Acceptance check: run `python scripts/check_eol.py && test -z \"$(git grep -Il $'\\r')\"` and assert both succeed in CI; also run `git diff --check` to catch whitespace errors. To make it falsifiable: if after the fix the command returns non-zero or any output, the fix is incomplete.",
